{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"community-needs-assessment-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Community needs assessment track","description":"Runs a community needs assessment for a nonprofit - scope the questions, plan fair data collection, analyse what was found, then decide what to do - pausing for approval at each stage.","category":"nonprofit","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["discover","plan","review"],"role":["manager","executive"],"subject":["nonprofit"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","dataset","notes"],"output":["plan","questions","report"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","depth":"specific","tags":["needs-assessment","community-engagement","service-planning","participatory-research","asset-mapping"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["build-theory-of-change","set-programme-eligibility-criteria","evaluate-program-outcomes"],"personas":["nonprofit-advisor"]},"args":[{"name":"community","description":"The community or population (place, group or both), what the organisation does now, why it is assessing needs now (new strategy, funding, demand change), and any decision the results will inform.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"resources","description":"Time, budget, staff and volunteers available, languages spoken in the community, and any existing data (public statistics, service records, past surveys).","type":"text","required":true}],"steps":[{"id":"scope","file":"steps/01-scope.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"needs-assessment/01-scope.md"},{"id":"collect","file":"steps/02-collect.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"needs-assessment/02-collection-plan.md"},{"id":"analyse","file":"steps/03-analyse.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"needs-assessment/03-findings.md"},{"id":"decide","file":"steps/04-decide.md","stage":"plan","gate":"none","artifact":"needs-assessment/04-decisions.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Runs a community needs assessment the way an experienced community development lead would: decide the questions and the decision the results feed, collect evidence fairly from existing data and from the people affected (including those who rarely answer surveys), analyse needs alongside the community's own strengths, and turn findings into a small number of decisions. Each step writes one document and stops for approval.\n\nCommunity: {{community}}\nResources: {{resources}}\n\nRules for every step:\n- Never invent statistics, quotes or findings. Mark missing data [needed] and say how to get it.\n- Look for strengths and existing services as well as needs, so the organisation does not duplicate what others already do well.\n- Reach people who are usually missed (non-native speakers, people without internet, disabled people, young people, people in crisis) and say how.\n- Keep personal data minimal and gather it with informed consent; follow local data protection and research ethics expectations, which vary by country.\n- Do not promise the community services the organisation cannot fund; say how results will be reported back.\n- End each document with open questions.","steps":[{"id":"scope","file":"steps/01-scope.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"needs-assessment/01-scope.md","text":"# Step 1: Scope\n\nAgree what the assessment is for before collecting anything.\n\n1. Name the decision the results will inform (for example whether to open a new service, which of three needs to prioritise, how to redesign an existing service) and who makes it.\n2. Write three to six assessment questions that serve that decision. Cut questions that are interesting but would not change it.\n3. Define the community: boundaries, sub-groups to look at separately, and who speaks for whom (residents, service users, partner organisations, local professionals).\n4. List what is already known: existing public data, service records, past consultations, and other organisations' reports, with gaps.\n5. Set what is out of scope and the timeline.\n\nSections: Purpose and decision, Assessment questions, Community and sub-groups, What we already know, Out of scope, Timeline, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"collect","file":"steps/02-collect.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"needs-assessment/02-collection-plan.md","text":"# Step 2: Plan data collection\n\nDesign how evidence will be gathered for each approved question.\n\n1. For each assessment question, pick methods that fit: existing data, a short survey, listening sessions or focus groups, interviews with key informants, community mapping of assets and services, observation or walk-arounds. Explain why each method suits the question.\n2. Draft the survey (no more than 12 questions, neutral wording, plain language, offered in the community's main languages and on paper and phone as well as online) and a discussion guide for listening sessions.\n3. Plan reach: where and how to meet each sub-group, partners who can host, incentives or expenses, accessibility, childcare and timing.\n4. Plan consent, anonymity, data storage and who sees raw data.\n5. Assign who does what, by when, within the stated resources. If the resources do not fit the plan, cut methods and say what is lost.\n\nSections: Methods by question, Survey draft, Listening session guide, Reaching people, Consent and data, Workplan, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"analyse","file":"steps/03-analyse.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"needs-assessment/03-findings.md","text":"# Step 3: Analyse\n\nMake sense of what was collected. Ask for the collected data first if it has not been shared; do not proceed on assumed results.\n\n1. Summarise who took part against who was meant to, and name which groups are under-represented and how that limits the findings.\n2. For each assessment question, give the findings with their sources, separating what many people said from single strong comments, and quantitative from qualitative evidence.\n3. Map assets: existing services, groups and strengths the community named.\n4. List needs that emerged, rated by size (how many affected), severity, how well others already meet them, and fit with the organisation's purpose and capacity.\n5. Note contradictions between sources and what would resolve them.\n\nSections: Who took part, Findings by question, Community assets, Needs ranked, Contradictions and limits, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"decide","file":"steps/04-decide.md","stage":"plan","gate":"none","artifact":"needs-assessment/04-decisions.md","text":"# Step 4: Decide and report back\n\nTurn approved findings into decisions and close the loop with the community.\n\n1. Recommend two or three priorities with reasons tied to the findings, and say explicitly which needs the organisation will not address and who might.\n2. For each priority: the response options (new service, partnership, advocacy, changing an existing service, referral), rough resources needed as items to cost, and the first steps.\n3. Draft a one-page summary to share back with the community in plain language, with what the organisation heard, what it will do and what it cannot do.\n4. Set measures to check whether the response meets the need, and when to repeat the assessment.\n\nSections: Priorities, Response options, Summary for the community, Measures and next review, Open questions."}]}
